#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""quarry term — the catalogue in a terminal: search, browse, play, save.

    quarry term [QUERY]                 browse full-screen (arrows, / to search)
    quarry term show QUERY [-n N]       print a strip of results into the scrollback
    quarry term cat ID|FILE|URL         one picture, large; animated ones play

Options (any command):
    --gfx blocks|kitty    how pictures are drawn (default: detected)
    --size N              tile width in columns (browse/show)
    --live                show: ask the sites first, not only the catalogue
    --loops N             cat: how many times an animation plays (0 = until a key)

$QUARRY_TERM_MPV adds options to mpv, which plays video and audio (p).

In zash inside zeit, browsing opens a stage beside the grid: a zeit web pane
that zash draws with Chrome, so the selected item is there in real pixels —
the original, GIFs moving, video playing. v hides and shows it;
QUARRY_TERM_STAGE=0 turns it off, =1 forces it.

WHY HALF-BLOCKS BY DEFAULT. Inside zeit, kitty graphics never reach zash: the
pane parser drops APC sequences, so `a=T` placements vanish. "▀" with a 24-bit
foreground and background is two pixels per cell, survives every multiplexer,
and animates by redrawing text. Kitty graphics are used where they are known to
arrive: kitty itself, and zash outside zeit and tmux.

Talks to quarryd over its socket with `find` (keeps no result, so the gallery
window's `queryId` is never invalidated), `more` (the same live site search the
app uses), `thumb`, `export` and nothing else.
"""

